    "gearArmorTraditionHEGEMONY": "Hegemony",
    "gearArmorTraditionINDEPENDENT": "Independent",
    "gearArmorTraditionKNIGHTS_OF_LUDD": "Knights of Ludd",
    "gearArmorTraditionLIONS_GUARD": "Lion's Guard",
    "gearArmorTraditionLUDDIC_CHURCH": "Luddic Church",
    "gearArmorTraditionLUDDIC_PATH": "Luddic Path",
    "gearArmorTraditionPERSEAN": "Persean League",
    "gearArmorTraditionPIRATES": "Pirates",
    "gearArmorTraditionSINDRIAN_DIKTAT": "Sindrian Diktat",
    "gearArmorTraditionTRITACHYON": "Tri-Tachyon",
    "gearCatalogArmorAegisCompositeDescription": "Tri-Tachyon's ARM-TACH-3-SUPP exchanges thick conventional plate for laminated composites, predictive threat displays, and powered balance correction. It cannot absorb a slugging match like ARM-HEG-3-LN armor, but it moves quickly and presents a difficult firing solution.",
    "gearCatalogArmorAegisCompositeDisplayName": "ARM-TACH-3-SUPP",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestDescription": "The Hegemony's answer to carrying the heavy thing: a powered suit whose frame takes the weight of whatever the section hands it. It is slower than an ARM-HEG-3-LN and worth every second of that, because it keeps walking under fire that stops a line trooper.",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-4-PWR (MSL)",
    "gearCatalogArmorBulwarkHeavyDescription": "The League's ARM-PRSLG-4-PWR program favors modular plate blocks, replaceable actuators, and control standards that member worlds can reproduce. It lacks any one spectacular advantage and instead preserves credible protection, handling, and coalition repairability together.",
    "gearCatalogArmorBulwarkHeavyDisplayName": "ARM-PRSLG-4-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchLineLore": "Gilead line suits are inherited machines kept under the Church's rules on technology: joint governors set conservatively, and every rebuild signed off by a commissary inspector. Hand-fitted ceramic makes the formation slow, but its unusually resistant plate rewards a section that holds a chosen line.",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchLineProvenance": "Wardens' Commissary, guard grade",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchWardensLore": "Parish wardens do not waste inherited machinery where common plate will serve. ARM-SEC-2-LN kits protect the ordinary defenders, while two maintained ARM-HEG-3-LN shells per squad go to the most experienced wardens, who are expected to stand against the first breach.",
    "gearCatalogArmorChurchWardensProvenance": "Wardens' Commissary, parish grade",
    "gearCatalogArmorCombatDescription": "Kazeron's Bastion pattern lets League member worlds replace one another's plates and seals. Layered laminate trades modest mobility for dependable coalition line protection.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCombatDisplayName": "ARM-PRSLG-3-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorCordonShellDescription": "ARM-SUR-1-LN shells are obsolete station-security cuirasses sold after their seals, shock liners, or liability tags fail inspection. A local armorer can make one wearable again, but mismatched plates and tired joints leave the user with protection no professional force would call dependable.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCordonShellDisplayName": "ARM-SUR-1-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateHeavyLore": "ARM-TACH-4-PWR battlesuits make a walking tank behave like corporate response equipment: high-output joints, composite protection, and continuous threat prediction over maximum plate. They cross danger quickly and remain difficult to settle sights on, but cannot trade punishment with ARM-HEG-4-PWR shells forever.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateHeavyProvenance": "Asset Protection, blacksite tier",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateLineLore": "ARM-TACH-3-SUPP composite armor refuses the low-tech premise that line infantry must become easy targets to survive. Predictive threat displays and powered balance correction preserve speed and reduce clean hostile solutions, paid for with less plate and a demanding maintenance train.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorporateLineProvenance": "Asset Protection, response tier",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorpsmanDescription": "Hegemony line establishments budget one trained corpsman per section and equip them properly: a powered harness that carries a surgeon's worth of consumables, keeps them cold, and leaves both hands free. The Navy's own figure for what it buys is a third of the casualties it would otherwise ship home.",
    "gearCatalogArmorCorpsmanDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-4-SUPP",
    "gearCatalogArmorFieldFatiguesDescription": "Domain-pattern vac fatigues remain the working uniform of crews, militias, and cash-poor infantry. Local factories preserve the seals; unrestricted movement comes at the price of dangerous exposure.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFieldFatiguesDisplayName": "ARM-VAC-1-UNP",
    "gearCatalogArmorFieldFatiguesLore": "The founding issue: twelve worn ARM-SUR-1-LN shells over field fatigues. Surplus plates provide basic protection, with no integral systems, scout sensors, assault shells, or support harnesses. New equipment specifications improve the issue and open those roles.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFleetCombatLore": "Hegemony protection tables privilege standardized, repairable plate over elegant integration. ARM-HEG-3-LN suits dominate the establishment and ARM-PRSLG-3-LN laminates fill the remaining powered billets, giving the whole squad the weight to take contact and keep its formation.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFleetCombatProvenance": "Ordnance Board line schedule",
    "gearCatalogArmorFoundryBreakerDescription": "Foundry-breakers begin as cargo exoskeletons and end beneath welded ship plate, illicit servos, and whatever shock padding a raider cell can steal. The crude mass takes extraordinary punishment before coming apart, but offers poor resistance and nearly no tactical grace.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFoundryBreakerDisplayName": "ARM-PIR-4-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorFurnaceLineDescription": "Sindrian ARM-SIND-3-LN armor surrounds a conventional pressure frame with thick petrochemical laminate and conspicuous state fittings. It carries more sacrificial plate than its peers, while heat, weight, and an unsubtle silhouette punish prolonged maneuver.",
    "gearCatalogArmorFurnaceLineDisplayName": "ARM-SIND-3-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorHeavyDescription": "The ARM-HEG-4-PWR heavy battlesuit comes out of the Battlegroup's own Domain-spec drawings and is released only on the Ordnance Board's restricted schedule: dense ceramic, powered bracing, and standardized hardpoints. It survives punishment at an obvious cost in speed.",
    "gearCatalogArmorHeavyDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-4-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyAuxiliaryLore": "The Hegemony expects auxiliaries to hold formation even when the quartermaster cannot furnish a regular battalion. ARM-SEC-2-LN kits cover the section while one ARM-HEG-3-LN suit per fire team anchors the billets most likely to receive contact.",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyAuxiliaryProvenance": "Ordnance Board auxiliary schedule",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyShockLore": "A complete ARM-HEG-4-PWR establishment is the Hegemony's answer to a breach that must become a held corridor, and the Ordnance Board releases it to few formations outside the Battlegroup's own. Dense Domain-spec ceramic and standardized powered bracing absorb sustained punishment, while the squad accepts a measured advance and an obvious target profile.",
    "gearCatalogArmorHegemonyShockProvenance": "XIV Battlegroup restricted schedule",
    "gearCatalogArmorKnightsHeavyLore": "ARM-LUDD-4-PWR battlesuits are rebuilt one shell at a time and signed out to a single Knight, who answers for its upkeep. Their dense fitted armor turns aside heavy fire better than most infantry systems, but immense mass and conservative joint governors demand patience from the wearer.",
    "gearCatalogArmorKnightsHeavyProvenance": "Knights' armoury, Hesperus",
    "gearCatalogArmorLashplateHarnessDescription": "An ARM-SALV-1-LN harness is a cargo frame, pressure web, and slabs of whatever a breaker crew could drill and wire into place. The mass can catch fragments and weak rounds, but crude balance, exposed lines, and almost nonexistent material matching make it cheap protection for expendable hands.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLashplateHarnessDisplayName": "ARM-SALV-1-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueHeavyLore": "ARM-PRSLG-4-PWR suits extend coalition interoperability into the assault role. Replaceable actuators and modular plate blocks give member worlds a heavy system they can actually sustain, producing credible protection and handling without relying on one inaccessible miracle component.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueHeavyProvenance": "Joint Procurement, breach grade",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueLineLore": "A full Bastion schedule is the League ideal: not identical national uniforms, but plates, seals, and powered joints that member worlds have agreed to replace. Its protection and handling stay deliberately balanced because coalition logistics punish brilliant exceptions.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueLineProvenance": "Joint Procurement, coalition grade",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueMobileLore": "A coalition screen values common interfaces more than a single world’s parade standard. Imported ARM-TACH-2-RCN suits give half the formation speed and threat awareness; locally replaceable ARM-SEC-2-LN kits keep the remainder useful when the imported weave needs a depot.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLeagueMobileProvenance": "Joint Procurement, member grade",
    "gearCatalogArmorLineDescription": "Built to an Ordnance Board pattern in the Chicomoztoc works, the Hegemony's ARM-HEG-3-LN suit favors interchangeable plates, pressure integrity, and field-serviceable joints. It is heavier than scout gear and built for disciplined formations far from a depot.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLineDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-3-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsGuardHeavyLore": "The ARM-LION-4-PWR is bought outside the Office of Supply's budget, by a procurement office that answers to the Executor rather than to a ministry. Oversized cooling loops, lavish support, thick laminate and a finish recognisable across a refinery concourse go with it, because a Guard section is also a statement about whom the regime trusts. The enormous reserve rewards a disciplined frontal advance; weight and visibility cost it speed and any chance of going unnoticed.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsGuardHeavyProvenance": "Lion's Guard procurement, by allotment",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsMantleDescription": "ARM-LION-4-PWR suits carry thick Sindrian laminates, oversized cooling loops, and a finish nobody is meant to mistake for regular issue. Allotment follows political favour as much as need, and the logistics behind it are lavish: an enormous armor reserve, with a weight and silhouette that leave the wearer dependent on a disciplined advance.",
    "gearCatalogArmorLionsMantleDisplayName": "ARM-LION-4-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorMilitiaDescription": "Ward is the frontier name for locally assembled torso plate, sealed helmet, and available impact layers. Stations, Church villages, and auxiliaries issue it to section leaders.",
    "gearCatalogArmorMilitiaDisplayName": "ARM-SEC-2-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorOrderlyDescription": "Parish infirmaries have kitted out stretcher parties for three centuries and see no reason to stop because the stretcher party is now under fire. The harness carries dressings, plasma and a folding litter where a weapons carrier would stow ammunition, and it is issued to whichever member of the section can be trusted to keep using it while being shot at.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOrderlyDisplayName": "ARM-LUDD-2-SUPP",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawDescription": "An ARM-PIR-2-LN rig means cut-down security armor, industrial exoframe, ship plate, and hand-fitted seals from pirate or Pather shops. No two suits agree; the better examples are quick and stubborn.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawDisplayName": "ARM-PIR-2-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawHeavyLore": "Foundry-breakers are cargo exoskeletons buried under stolen servos and welded ship plate. Crude mass lets them keep moving after cleaner suits would fail, but poor resistance, brutal handling, and a vast target profile make every assault a wager against concentrated fire.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawHeavyProvenance": "Pather and pirate foundry cells",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawLineLore": "An ARM-PIR-3-LN is what remains when a patchwork frame survives enough raids to earn powered bracing and still more ship plate. Veteran crews get speed and an intimidating reserve of material, but mismatched layers provide poor resistance to a serious penetrator.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawLineProvenance": "Veteran outlaw warbands",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawRaidersLore": "Blackforge shops cut security shells and cargo frames down for fast violent work, then issue the results to almost everyone expected to reach the target. The final pair receive sealed fatigues, a weapon, and no illusion that recovery is part of the plan.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutlawRaidersProvenance": "Pirate and Pather raiders",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutriderDescription": "One League member world builds the shell, another the array, a third the endurance pack, and the coalition argues about the interfaces for a decade. What comes out is a scout suit a section can live in for a week, and that puts everything it sees on a net six navies can read.",
    "gearCatalogArmorOutriderDisplayName": "ARM-PRSLG-4-RCN",
    "gearCatalogArmorPackframeDescription": "An unpowered cargo frame with the load arms cut short and plate hung where the crates used to ride. Independent crews build them because the frame was already in the hold and it carries more than a marine can on their own back.",
    "gearCatalogArmorPackframeDisplayName": "ARM-INDP-2-ASLT",
    "gearCatalogArmorPalatineDescription": "Church workshops on Gilead rebuild these suits from inherited Domain pressure shells, hand-fitted ceramic, and carefully husbanded powered joints, and every rebuild is entered in the commissary's books. The result is slow and laborious to maintain, but unusually resistant once its wearer sets their feet.",
    "gearCatalogArmorPalatineDisplayName": "ARM-LUDD-3-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorPathfinderDescription": "The Hegemony does not buy corporate sensor suits; it strips an ARM-HEG-3-LN down to the frame and hangs a surveying array off it. The ARM-HEG-3-RCN is heavier, louder, and far easier to fix than anything Tri-Tachyon sells a scout, and an auxiliary can be taught to run one in a fortnight.",
    "gearCatalogArmorPathfinderDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-3-RCN",
    "gearCatalogArmorReaverDescription": "An ARM-PIR-3-LN is a patchwork frame that survived long enough to acquire powered bracing and another generation of ship plate. It is fast for its bulk and can soak punishment, but poor material matching gives penetrators few reasons to stop.",
    "gearCatalogArmorReaverDisplayName": "ARM-PIR-3-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorReconLore": "ARM-TACH-2-RCN scout suits equip the operators expected to see the enemy, survive the first exchange, and disappear before heavier forces arrive. Cheaper ARM-SEC-2-LN backing leaves the expensive sensor weave concentrated where corporate doctrine expects the best return.",
    "gearCatalogArmorReconProvenance": "Asset Protection, contract tier",
    "gearCatalogArmorRedoubtDescription": "Hegemony siege issue: ARM-HEG-3-LN plate with every weight limit taken off, because the marine wearing it is not expected to go anywhere. It is what holds a breach once the assault sections have made one, and it is miserable to walk any distance in.",
    "gearCatalogArmorRedoubtDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-4-LN",
    "gearCatalogArmorReliquaryHeavyDescription": "The Knights' armoury on Hesperus rebuilds each ARM-LUDD-4-PWR by hand from a legacy Domain heavy shell, and signs it out only to a Knight who has passed the armoury's handling and maintenance trials. Dense fitted armor turns aside heavy fire, but conservative governors and immense mass make the wearer deliberate rather than swift.",
    "gearCatalogArmorReliquaryHeavyDisplayName": "ARM-LUDD-4-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorRiotShellDescription": "Corporate security surplus, sold by the container to anyone who will take it. It was built to walk a picket line into a crowd, so it has a real shield and almost no assist worth the name -- and it turns out a station corridor is not so different from a picket line.",
    "gearCatalogArmorRiotShellDisplayName": "ARM-TACH-2-ASLT",
    "gearCatalogArmorScoutDescription": "Tri-Tachyon's ARM-TACH-2-RCN suit hides composite segments beneath a sensor hood and electronic-warfare weave. Corporate scouts prize its speed; gray-market users struggle with the interfaces.",
    "gearCatalogArmorScoutDisplayName": "ARM-TACH-2-RCN",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianLineLore": "ARM-SIND-3-LN armor is Askonia's fuel industry worked into plate. Thick sacrificial laminate gives every soldier a deep protection reserve, while heat, ordinary resistance materials, and a conspicuous silhouette make maneuver and incoming fire less forgiving.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianLineProvenance": "Office of Supply, regular grade",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianSecurityLore": "A mixed protection schedule descended from refinery guards and planetary auxiliaries. Imported ARM-TACH-2-RCN suits go to mobile response billets, ARM-SEC-2-LN kits go where cameras are watching, and the rest receive sealed fatigues beneath a very visible uniform.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSindrianSecurityProvenance": "Office of Supply, security grade",
    "equipmentTemplateStorySourceRoute": "Acquisition belongs to an authored story reward. Ordinary market, license, patron and recovery channels do not offer this technology.",
    "debugPowerSuitHeading": "Power-suit test technology",
    "debugPowerSuitLearningNote": "These developer actions permanently learn two armor patterns in this save. Fit them manually through Barracks > Equipment; current personnel and kit stay as issued.",
    "debugPowerSuitLearnRecovered": "Learn recovered suit test patterns",
    "debugPowerSuitLearnMilitary": "Learn military suit test patterns",
    "debugPowerSuitLearned": "Learned {0} new armor patterns. Issue its Equipment card from Barracks > Equipment.",
    "debugPowerSuitNoRoster": "Marine roster is unavailable; no technology learned.",
    "loadoutCorporateSpecterSectionName": "Tri-Tachyon Specter Section",
    "loadoutCorporateSpecterSectionDescription": "Two ARM-TACH-3-PWR (QS) powered breachers in the assault seats, a scout, two support gunners in ARM-TACH-3-SUPP and seven riflemen in combat armor. A first, light taste of powered armor: ten of twelve marines stay in ordinary kit.",
    "gearCatalogCorporateSpecterSectionProvenance": "Tri-Tachyon field trial",
    "gearCatalogCorporateSpecterSectionLore": "Tri-Tachyon sells prediction before it sells plate, and its first offer is two suits in a section of ordinary marines. The ARM-TACH-3-PWR (QS) frames are a tier below the ARM-TACH-4-PWR, which keeps the price of entry reasonable. The card carries the whole section, scouts and riflemen included.",
    "loadoutHegemonyArbalestSectionName": "Hegemony Arbalest Section",
    "loadoutHegemonyArbalestSectionDescription": "Two ARM-HEG-3-PWR (QS) powered support suits, with an ARM-HEG-3-RCN scout and ten riflemen in ARM-HEG-3-LN. The rest of the section stays in ordinary Hegemony kit.",
    "loadoutHegemonyAuxiliarySectionName": "Hegemony Auxiliary Section",
    "loadoutHegemonyAuxiliarySectionDescription": "Four ARM-HEG-4-PWR (MSL) powered support suits, with ARM-HEG-3-RCN scouts and riflemen in ARM-HEG-3-LN. Issued only under a Hegemony military warrant.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsTrenchClearingProvenance": "Freeport security surplus",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsTrenchClearingLore": "What a security contractor issues the week somebody digs in across a road: the same rifles as always, four old rocket tubes for the guns at the ends of the line, and a crate of grenades for the trench itself. It will not take a line held by regulars, but it gives a dockside company a chance against one held by people like themselves.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsCheckpointProvenance": "Jangala road contracts",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsCheckpointLore": "What a road contractor issues once the first fight is won and the job becomes keeping it: the same rifles, a sapper in every team with a satchel charge, and smoke for the minutes it takes to set one. It holds the road by clearing what overlooks it.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsConvoyEscortProvenance": "Supply-route escort stores",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsConvoyEscortLore": "Issued to the people who walk beside the trucks. Every team carries a rocket tube for whatever stops the column, and one marine humps a DEP-PD-GUN that can be set down over the road when the rockets start coming the other way.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsCounterBatteryProvenance": "Hegemony garrison issue, Corvus",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsCounterBatteryLore": "A Hegemony counter-mortar table from the garrison wars of the last century: two MBL-PD-MLS packs to take the rounds out of the air, and one anti-materiel rifle to go and find the tube. The licence was never withdrawn, but the packs stopped being made, so a market lists it only when some depot clears its shelves.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsBuildersGuardProvenance": "Shepherd yard stores, Asharu",
    "gearCatalogWeaponsBuildersGuardLore": "An old colonial works-guard schedule, written for survey crews raising the first domes. Three sappers with satchel charges and two DEP-PD-GUNs: a guard detail for ground somebody is building on, which is to say ground somebody else wants to shell. The pods are long out of production, and sets surface a few at a time from closed yards.",
    "gearCatalogFreeportAssaultProvenance": "Independent militia contracts",
    "gearCatalogFreeportAssaultLore": "Freeport militias cannot buy assault plate, so they hang it on cargo frames instead. Two packframes take the front of the section while ten marines in militia plate close behind them; it is the cheapest section on the contract boards that looks heavy, and its weapons card decides whether it can open a defended line.",
    "gearCatalogHegemonyArbalestSectionProvenance": "Hegemony auxiliary salvage",
    "gearCatalogHegemonyArbalestSectionLore": "Auxiliary sections get the Hegemony's recovered fire-support frames long before they see its assault plate. Two suits give a section somebody who can keep walking under fire; everybody else keeps marching in the standard line armor.",
    "gearCatalogHegemonyAuxiliarySectionProvenance": "Hegemony auxiliary issue, under warrant",
    "gearCatalogHegemonyAuxiliarySectionLore": "An auxiliary schedule the Hegemony has issued unchanged for a hundred years: current ARM-HEG-4-PWR (MSL) frames with their seals intact and their maintenance logs signed. An auxiliary gets the support suit first. The assault armour comes when the terms have been kept.",
    "loadoutSalvageAssistedName": "Salvage-assisted Section",
    "loadoutSalvageAssistedDescription": "Four recovered power suits, two ARM-TACH-3-PWR (QS) breachers and two ARM-HEG-3-PWR (QS) support gunners, among a scout and seven riflemen in militia armor. The whole section comes with the card; the suits are a third of the squad.",
    "gearCatalogSalvageAssistedProvenance": "Company salvage doctrine",
    "gearCatalogSalvageAssistedLore": "Recovered powered frames are concentrated in the breach and support billets while militia-grade scouts and riflemen fill out the section. It is the last step before a company fields whole sections in powered armor.",
    "loadoutBoardingJackSectionName": "Boarding Jack Section",
    "loadoutBoardingJackSectionDescription": "Two rebuilt ARM-PIR-3-PWR frames in the assault seats, a scout, two support gunners in packframes and seven raiders in outlaw plate. Ten of twelve marines stay in ordinary kit; the frames go through the hatch first.",
    "gearCatalogBoardingJackSectionProvenance": "Pirate boarding crews",
    "gearCatalogBoardingJackSectionLore": "Boarding crews put their two heaviest frames on the hatch and everyone else behind them. The card carries the whole section, scouts and raiders included. Mass soaks a scattered volley; it does not stand up to a disciplined line with anti-armor weapons, and the crews that forget that do not keep their frames.",
    "gearCatalogArmorBoardingJackDisplayName": "ARM-PIR-3-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorBoardingJackDescription": "A loading frame rebuilt for boarding: compact pressure helmet, welded mismatched plate over visible joint backing, guarded hoses and hand-lettered service labels. Heavy mass absorbs scattered small-arms hits, but low resistance and poor handling punish exposure to military weapons. A bigger raw reserve does not make it a better ARM-TACH-3-PWR (QS).",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterRecoveredDisplayName": "ARM-TACH-3-PWR (QS)",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterRecoveredDescription": "A rebuilt corporate breach shell with mismatched composite panels and tired actuators. Its patched armor still outlasts regular infantry kit, but reduced protection and a slower predictive breach cycle leave a clear step to military issue.",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestRecoveredDisplayName": "ARM-HEG-3-PWR (QS)",
    "gearCatalogArmorArbalestRecoveredDescription": "A salvaged support shell with replacement plate and a worn load-bearing frame. Its reduced armor reserve leaves a clear step to the military pattern.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterHeavyDescription": "The ARM-TACH-4-PWR is a corporate breach shell built around high-output joints, composite armor, and continuous threat prediction. It gives up the ARM-HEG-4-PWR's raw staying power to cross exposed ground faster and remain unexpectedly elusive for a walking tank.",
    "gearCatalogArmorSpecterHeavyDisplayName": "ARM-TACH-4-PWR",
    "gearCatalogArmorVigilDescription": "Diktat state security orders these by the crate for work its own citizens are not supposed to hear about. It is a riot shell built up until it can take a rifle round rather than a thrown brick -- heavier than the pattern it descends from, lighter than anything the Lion's Guard would deign to wear, and issued to people expected to go through a door on a schedule.",
    "gearCatalogArmorVigilDisplayName": "ARM-SIND-3-ASLT",
    "gearCatalogBulwarkDescription": "A League siege walker built the way the coalition builds everything: replaceable actuators, shared control standards, and a hull thick enough that its faults can be argued about at leisure. Member worlds assemble it under licence from Kazeron drawings, and enough sets have been sold, captured and rebuilt since that no two are quite the same machine. It shares its name with the coalition battlesuit, and its crews will tell you it shares the temperament.",
    "gearCatalogBulwarkRole": "Heavy line chassis",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechAc6Description": "The AC-6, the gun Hegemony crews still call the Bastion: the League's interchangeable autocannon in its arm fit, sharing feed geometry and control standards with half the coalition's other hardware. Kazeron sells the drawings as readily as the guns, and the Hegemony issues it to its walkers by the regiment. Six heavy rounds a burst off a ten-round belt, reaching further and biting deeper than any commercial gun of its size. Interoperability rather than brilliance is the point of it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDc5Description": "The DC-5, the Foundry Breaker: a short mortar bored out of mining stock and hung where an arm gun should be, meant for walls rather than for people. Pather foundry cells build them from whatever the last yard yielded, which is why no two share a bore. Five shells in the bin and every one of them is spent on architecture.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechChaingunDescription": "A Domain-pattern rotary cannon hung off a walker's arm or under a light chassis's nose, the gun every arsenal reaches for when it wants a machine able to answer everything at once. The mechanism is old enough that no faction claims it and common enough that none of them guards it. Heavy frames carry one on each arm; crews call the second the argument settler.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechChaingunHfDescription": "The same rotary cannon on a military drive and a deeper hopper: the barrels come up to speed in half the time, the feed keeps pace with a long hold, and the rounds are loaded heavier, every fourth one a red tracer. Line yards build them for crews who have learned what the stock gun does when its belt runs out.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechHc10Description": "The HC-10: the heavy cannon's short sibling, one large bore on a breech light enough to hang on a walker's side arm. It throws a single armour-piercing shell a little further than the HC-20 and hits a good deal softer, which makes it the gun a crew fits when the heavy cannon will not go where the work is. Military yards build it to the same drawings as its big brother, and the same ammunition fits neither.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechAc6QsDescription": "The QuickSell AC-6, the Muster to the convoys that run it: an autocannon for militia walkers that could not afford a Bastion. To get the price down the maker built it round a commodity receiver and an open feed, dropped the long barrel and the hardened rounds, and let the tolerances go. It still fires six to a burst off a ten-round tray, faster than the real thing and nowhere near as hard, and most of them miss. Cheaper, smaller and worse is the QuickSell promise, and the gun keeps it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechPl2ApDescription": "The PL-2 (AP): an emitter array from the same Tri-Tachyon security family as the AR-PULSE, scaled up until it needs a walker's power plant to matter. Its eight hard, tightly focused pulses reach further than any other pulse laser and pierce plate the others only scorch, the energy arm a crew fits for armour. Corporate response teams field them; everyone else buys them second-hand and complains about the diagnostics. Nothing about the pattern is subtle, the light included.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm5FragDescription": "The same small rack loaded with fragmentation rockets: a wide burst of steel that clears a trench and barely marks a hull. Militia and pirate shops pack their own, which is why the cassettes rarely match.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechHc20Description": "The HC-20: a single large-bore arm gun of Hegemony manufacture, built to put one deliberate shell into something that had earned it. It is the longest continuously produced mech weapon in circulation and the easiest anywhere to find ammunition for. Its crews are unhurried people.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm15FragDescription": "The heavy shoulder rack loaded for men rather than machines. Each salvo blankets a doorway in fragments; against plate it is mostly noise.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLrm5FragDescription": "A small artillery rack firing airburst fragmentation over cover. Garrison batteries prefer it for keeping infantry heads down at a distance.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLrm15FragDescription": "The full artillery rack with a fragmentation load, the support walker's answer to massed infantry. It scatters men across a block and leaves a tank to someone else.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLrm15Description": "The full artillery rack: boosted missiles on a shoulder mount, and the reason a support walker is built with its feet so far apart. Diktat and League batteries field them in pairs, and the drawings have leaked far enough that a pirate band occasionally manages one. A machine carrying two is not expected to be anywhere near the fighting. Its tandem warheads are shaped charges for plate; the rack holds fire at infantry.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLrm5Description": "A small indirect-fire rack that lobs its missiles over whatever stands in the way, using guidance a light chassis has no other use for. It is standard fire-support fitment across the coalition and the Diktat and circulates freely by salvage besides. Its crews rarely see what they hit. Its tandem warheads are shaped charges for plate; the rack holds fire at infantry.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechPl3Description": "The PL-3: the laser tank's gun taken off its turret ring and built into an arm: a glass orb lens on a bulky white capacitor block that dumps its charge in four quick pulses and then has to vent. It out-trades almost anything its size for one second and then goes quiet for several, clearing infantry and light machines while it only scores heavy plate. Walkers that carry one plan their fights around the vent.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechBl2Description": "The BL-2: the beam laser every midline frigate carries, in an arm mount: a glass orb lens on a white electronics block with a red-banded collar. Hold the trigger and it holds a continuous beam on the cursor, steady enough to follow a drone across the sky, for as long as the frame has the flux to feed it. It is the answer to fliers and light machines: it bites light plate well enough, but the heavy plate of a battle tank shrugs it off, and it leaves missiles to the hull's point defence.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechPl3SweepDescription": "The PL-3 (Sweep): the largest red-banded laser, a glass orb lens at the front of a bulky white arm that carries its own electronics and cooling block. It lays fourteen light pulses on a target a pull at a time and runs cool enough to do it again and again, sweeping infantry and fliers off a street while it does next to nothing to plate. The old PD laser tank carried this gun on its turret ring.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechPl2Description": "The PL-2, the middle pulse laser: a glass orb lens on a compact white capacitor housing that fires three quick pulses and vents. It hits harder than a beam for a moment and then has to rest, a working arm against infantry, fliers and light machines.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechPl1Description": "The PL-1, the smallest pulse laser: a glass orb lens on a slim white housing that snaps off two hard pulses and vents. A sidearm for machines with one spare row, it picks off infantry and fliers between a heavier gun's shots.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLightMgDescription": "A light machine gun on a plain receiver: a belt, a short barrel and a feed tray, sized for a technical's pintle or a walker's forearm rather than a hardpoint of its own. It does not threaten armor and does not try to. It is what a small mount carries when the mount is all the gun there is room for.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechBl1Description": "The BL-1, the smallest beam laser: a glass orb lens and white electronics cut down to a slim, light arm mount. Hold the trigger and it holds a thin continuous beam on the cursor, a tracking beam that shreds drones, fliers and men in the open and barely marks a machine's plate. Frigate crews still call it a point-defence laser, though it has never shot down a missile. It fits any machine with a spare one-row mount.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm3QsDescription": "The QuickSell SRM-3: a smaller, cheaper short-range rack for militia and security units that could not afford a sealed SRM. To get the price down the maker cut the design back to what could be stamped and welded in bulk. The boost motor, the armoured warhead fuse and the sealed housing were all taken out, and the tubes ride an open rail on loose tolerances. It throws three rockets where a sealed SRM-5 throws five, and they fly straight, scatter and carry no impact charge. Cheaper, smaller and worse is the QuickSell promise, and the rack keeps it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechDc4QsDescription": "The QuickSell DC-4, the Quarry breaker on the yards that bought it: a demolition cannon for contractors who could not get a Foundry Breaker. To get the price down the maker took a mining projector, cut the bore, the shell and the bin down to four rounds, and put no work into the sights at all. It knocks walls down slower than the DC-5, from closer, and its accuracy is a matter of local opinion. Cheaper, smaller and worse is the QuickSell promise, and the cannon keeps it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechLl3Description": "The LL-3, the Lance Cannon to the crews that fire it: a single heavy emitter on a shoulder yoke, drawing straight off the reactor and dispensing with ammunition altogether. One long lance a trigger burns through a body and into whatever stands behind it, the shot a walker saves for armour and walls. Tri-Tachyon built the pattern for facilities that could not be resupplied; everyone else fits it to machines they mean to keep in the field indefinitely. Flux rather than supply is what limits it.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm15Description": "The heavy shoulder rack of the same sealed family, three cells deep and fed from a bin the chassis has to be designed around. Coalition and Hegemony formations issue them, wealthy independents buy them, and everyone else finds one still attached to something they had to kill first. It is a doorway weapon and it is unapologetic about it. Its tandem warheads are shaped charges for plate; the rack holds fire at infantry.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechSrm5Description": "A small sealed short-range missile rack, the lightest a shoulder hardpoint will accept. Every arsenal in the sector builds a version, and the sealed cassette is what separates a military rack from a rail with rockets on it. A light chassis carries one and treats it as its whole argument up close. Its tandem warheads are shaped charges for plate; the rack holds fire at infantry.",
    "gearCatalogComponentMechThermalLanceDescription": "Lion's Brand, a Sindrian prestige emitter fitted where a missile rack would go. Lion's Guard formations receive them by allotment from their own procurement office, and diverted or captured examples are the only ones anybody else sees. It is expensive, conspicuous, and entirely serious.",
    "gearCatalogHoundDescription": "A light frame that began as a frontier survey walker and lost its instrument mast to somebody's cutting torch. Independent yards on a dozen worlds build the pattern out of commodity running gear, which is why it turns up in militia sheds, pirate holds and contractor manifests without anyone being able to say whose it was. It is quick, cheap to keep walking, and honest about how little it will absorb.",
    "gearCatalogHoundRole": "Light raider chassis",
    "gearCatalogWardenDescription": "A Sindrian-derived support walker laid out around a stable firing platform and two shoulder racks, exported in quantity because the Diktat would rather sell the machine than the doctrine for using it. The shoulders are its purpose; the arm mount exists so that the thing is not helpless once the racks run dry. Crews outside the Diktat learn its habits from salvage and from each other.",
    "gearCatalogWardenRole": "Fire support chassis",
    "gearCatalogSpecialAntiMaterielRifleDescription": "Tri-Tachyon's GR-AMR drives a magnetic heavy round through armor seams without endangering nearby friendlies. It carries five shots, depends on proprietary cells, and cannot breach walls.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialAntiMaterielRifleDisplayName": "GR-AMR",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFragGrenadeDescription": "Kazeron adopted the GRN-FRAG; leaked specifications now feed Diktat, pirate, and Pather arsenals. Its compact blast breaks exposed infantry but barely troubles walls or hardened emplacements.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFragGrenadeDisplayName": "GRN-FRAG",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFragGrenadeSubtitle": "Limited-ammunition anti-personnel ordnance",
    "gearCatalogSpecialIncendiaryGrenadeDisplayName": "GRN-INC",
    "gearCatalogSpecialIncendiaryGrenadeSubtitle": "Two thermite charges that set the ground alight",
    "gearCatalogSpecialIncendiaryGrenadeDescription": "A thermite and gel charge for burning out cover and clearing brush. It does little to a body it lands on; it does a great deal to the grass, hedges and growth around it, and to anyone who stays there.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFlamerDisplayName": "FL-THRM",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFlamerSubtitle": "Short-range projector for lighting a fire line",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFlamerDescription": "A backpack tank and a projector that throws burning gel a few cells. It is a tool for setting fires on purpose, a burn line or a hedge with someone behind it, and it is as dangerous to the squad that carries it as the fire it starts.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponIncendiaryGrenadeDisplayName": "GRN-INC",
    "gearCatalogWeaponFlamerDisplayName": "FL-THRM",
    "gearCatalogSpecialCommsRadioDescription": "A pack set and handset for a forward observer. While its carrier is alive, unsuppressed and sure of the ground, it lets them direct their side's fighters and bombers onto it. A strike over a strong defence is likely to meet anti-air.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialCommsRadioDisplayName": "MBL-CAS-Radio",
    "gearCatalogSpecialCommsRadioSubtitle": "Directs the air pool onto designated ground",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefenceEmplacementDescription": "A Gilead voidwright shipping-lane pod cut down for ground work: a cluster gun on a folding tripod that shreds incoming warheads and nothing else. It carries the rounds you brought, runs its cell flat, and can be shot like anything else standing in the open.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefenceEmplacementDisplayName": "DEP-PD-GUN",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefenceEmplacementSubtitle": "Placed, self-expiring ordnance denial",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefencePackDescription": "Hegemony issue for a squad that has to keep walking under shellfire: six interceptor missiles in an armoured launch block on a pack frame. It arms itself when warheads come in, and each interceptor climbs out of its tube, lights a second motor and runs onto the round where it will be. Close in, a mortar shell can land before one arrives, and six is not a barrage's worth; it thins the fire over a moving section rather than refusing it.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefencePackDisplayName": "MBL-PD-MLS",
    "gearCatalogSpecialPointDefencePackSubtitle": "Worn, self-arming ordnance cover for a moving squad",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldMortarDescription": "A two-piece infantry mortar of Hegemony pattern: a smoothbore tube, a baseplate and a bipod, carried by the gunner while a loader and a spotter carry the rounds and the eyes. It is set down where the section has halted, lobs high-angle shells onto ground it has been shown and cannot see, and is lifted again when the work is done or the enemy closes. It has a dead zone inside its minimum range and a small magazine, and a section that loses its gunner loses the tube.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldMortarDisplayName": "DEP-MRT",
    "gearCatalogSpecialFieldMortarSubtitle": "Crew-served indirect fire, set down and lifted again",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm1Description": "The Hegemony cut an old Domain rocket cassette into a disposable shoulder cluster, then every low-tech arsenal copied it. It breaches walls and hardened emplacements without distinguishing friend from foe.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm1DisplayName": "SRM-1",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm1Subtitle": "Limited-ammunition support weapon",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm3PodDisplayName": "SRM-3 (Pod)",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm3PodSubtitle": "Three self-aimed salvos from the shoulder",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm3PodDescription": "Tri-Tachyon bolted the micro-missile pod onto its support suits; every salvage yard since has unbolted it and strapped it to whatever shoulder was nearest. It finds its own target at standoff and puts three missiles into it while the wearer keeps shooting, and its splash does not ask whose side anyone is on.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSatchelChargeDescription": "Gilead voidwrights built the DEP-CHG for sealed machinery and hull work; Knights maintain originals while Pathers and pirates copy them. Successful planting starts its recharge cycle.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSatchelChargeDisplayName": "DEP-CHG",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSatchelChargeSubtitle": "Reusable cooldown-gated demolition utility",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm1QsDescription": "A workshop-made shoulder tube firing surplus demolition rockets. Poor sights, a slow reload and a weak shaped charge make it a threat to freight haulers rather than military armor.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm1QsDisplayName": "SRM-1 (QS)",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSrm1QsSubtitle": "Two crude unguided rounds",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSmokeGrenadeDescription": "League quartermasters built GRN-SMK to defeat incompatible coalition optics. Its dense multispectral cloud harms no one, blocks both sides equally, and buys only seconds to move.",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSmokeGrenadeDisplayName": "GRN-SMK",
    "gearCatalogSpecialSmokeGrenadeSubtitle": "Limited-ammunition tactical utility",
    "gearCatalogSystemCorpsmanKitDescription": "The same act the parish orderly performs, done by somebody taught how and carrying more to do it with. A trauma kit makes its wearer a squad medic who also treats the merely hurt, closes wounds faster and refills sooner.",
    "gearCatalogSystemCorpsmanKitDisplayName": "MED-KIT-Trauma",
    "gearCatalogSystemShieldLowTechDescription": "A salvaged riot screen and a shove, wired to one trigger. It throws the wearer forward behind a narrow sheet of scrap, holds for a moment, and needs a long rest before it will do it again.",
    "gearCatalogSystemShieldLowTechDisplayName": "SHLD-LT",
    "gearCatalogSystemShieldMidTechDescription": "Service-issue screen projector paired with the suit's own actuators: a wider arc that soaks more, a steadier push across open ground, and a recovery a section can plan around.",
    "gearCatalogSystemShieldMidTechDisplayName": "SHLD-MT",
    "gearCatalogSystemShieldHighTechDescription": "Military screen hardware on a doctrine timer. The broadest, deepest shield any suit fields, held longest, carrying the wearer through the threshold with the frontal arc locked forward the whole way.",
    "gearCatalogSystemShieldHighTechDisplayName": "SHLD-HT",
    "gearCatalogSystemParishAidDescription": "Kneel down, find the hole, close it, and get them back on their feet. It is not sophisticated and it does not need to be: most of what puts a marine out of a fight is bleeding, and most of that is answered by somebody willing to stop and deal with it. The kit makes its wearer a squad medic.",
    "gearCatalogSystemParishAidDisplayName": "MED-KIT",
    "gearCatalogWaylineForwardScreenLore": "Wayline's convoy scouts learned to value a clear approach over another layer of plate. Six Milspec Lancers and six dependable Rooks equip a company that wants to see trouble early. Castle pairs the first issue with a scout-heavy armor plan; the weapon definition remains reusable with other plans.",
    "gearCatalogWaylineSteadyLineLore": "Wayline's convoy-guard pattern from its first charter, kept on the books because nobody ever found a reason to change it. Three carefully maintained MG-SUPP automatics give each fire team an anchor; nine familiar GR-RFLs keep ammunition and replacement parts manageable for a small company. Castle teaches the first issue against a line-holding armor plan.",
    "gearCatalogWaylineSteadyLineProvenance": "Wayline cooperative",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSrm1DisplayName": "SRM-1",
    "gearCatalogWeaponAntiMaterielRifleDisplayName": "GR-AMR",
    "gearCatalogWeaponDmrDescription": "Kazeron combines revived the RL-DMR as a League interoperability project. Its standardized cells drive a narrow penetrator down sight lines ordinary rifles cannot contest.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponDmrDisplayName": "RL-DMR",
    "gearCatalogWeaponDmrRole": "MARKSMAN",
    "gearCatalogWeaponDronePulseDescription": "Tri-Tachyon designed the Wisp for tireless facility-security drones. Its measured cadence of two energy bolts favors persistent pressure; human-compatible stocks were never part of the contract.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponDronePulseDisplayName": "PE-1 Pulse Emitter",
    "gearCatalogWeaponDronePulseRole": "DRONE",
    "gearCatalogWeaponFieldRifleDescription": "Preserved in countless Domain forge archives, the GR-RFL serves Hegemony auxiliaries, Church militias, and frontier guards. Its heavy slug rewards steady aim; the slow action punishes a collapsing line.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponFieldRifleDisplayName": "GR-RFL",
    "gearCatalogWeaponFieldRifleRole": "LINE",
    "gearCatalogWeaponFragGrenadeDisplayName": "GRN-FRAG",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechAc6DisplayName": "AC-6 Autocannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechChaingunDisplayName": "Chaingun",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechChaingunHfDisplayName": "High-feed chaingun",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechLrmDisplayName": "LRM",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechLrmFragDisplayName": "LRM (Frag)",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechDc5DisplayName": "DC-5 Demolition Cannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechHc20DisplayName": "HC-20 Heavy Cannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechHc10DisplayName": "HC-10 Cannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechAc6QsDisplayName": "AC-6 (QS) Autocannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechPl3DisplayName": "PL-3 Pulse Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechBl2DisplayName": "BL-2 Beam Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechPl3SweepDisplayName": "PL-3 (Sweep) Pulse Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechPl2DisplayName": "PL-2 Pulse Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechPl1DisplayName": "PL-1 Pulse Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechLightMgDisplayName": "Light Machine Gun",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechBl1DisplayName": "BL-1 Beam Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechSrm3QsDisplayName": "SRM-3 (QS)",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechPl2ApDisplayName": "PL-2 (AP) Pulse Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechDc4QsDisplayName": "DC-4 (QS) Demolition Cannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechLl3DisplayName": "LL-3 Lance Laser",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechSrmDisplayName": "SRM",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechSrmFragDisplayName": "SRM (Frag)",
    "gearCatalogWeaponMechThermalLanceDisplayName": "Thermal Lance",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSrm3PodDisplayName": "SRM-3 (Pod)",
    "gearCatalogWeaponPdInterceptorDisplayName": "Interceptor Cluster",
    "gearCatalogWeaponPdPackDisplayName": "Interceptor Missile",
    "gearCatalogWeaponTankApsDisplayName": "Hull Active Protection",
    "gearCatalogWeaponPulseRifleDescription": "Tri-Tachyon sold the AR-PULSE as a corporate-security arm before the AI Wars made its logo awkward. Proprietary cells deliver flexible three-pulse fire at a corporate-maintenance price.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponPulseRifleDisplayName": "AR-PULSE",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSrm1QsDisplayName": "SRM-1 (QS)",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSmgDescription": "The GR-SMG is a black-market family, not one factory pattern. Pirate shops and Pather cells rebuild cheap pistol-calibre slug actions into compact automatics that empty a magazine across a boarding corridor in seconds.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSmgDisplayName": "GR-SMG",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSmgRole": "ASSAULT",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSquadAutomaticDescription": "The Hegemony standardized the MG-SUPP from a Domain support rifle. Its reinforced receiver sustains long slug bursts while League and Diktat arsenals copy it freely.",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSquadAutomaticDisplayName": "MG-SUPP",
    "gearCatalogWeaponSquadAutomaticRole": "SUPPORT",
    "gearCatalogWeaponTankTwinCannonDisplayName": "Breach Cannon",
    "gearCatalogWeaponTankPl3DisplayName": "PL-3 Pulse Laser Turret",
